    {
      "beat_id": "B01",
      "estimated_duration_s": 12,
      "narration_text": "This is Liam, in for Bear. A qubit can suffer infinitely many small rotations and disturbances. Why can a code focus on four symbols — I, X, Y, and Z? Because those four form a basis for single-qubit operators.",
      "shot": {
        "type": "CARD",
        "source": "remotion",
        "motion": "type",
        "remotion": {
          "pattern": "ClaudeComposerAsk",
          "props": {
            "command": "Explain why four Pauli errors can span continuous qubit errors.",
            "topic": "QUANTUM MECHANICS — ERROR CORRECTION",
            "segment": "A Four-Operator Basis",
            "greeting": "Namaste, Liam",
            "runningText": "expanding one-qubit operators…",
            "output": [
              "Operator basis: I, X, Y, Z.",
              "Correctable linear spans inherit correction."
            ],
            "folderLabel": "@NikBearBrown",
            "modelLabel": "Claude",
            "effortLabel": "High"
          },
          "rendered": {
            "out": "media/B01.mp4",
            "at": ""
          }
        }
      },
      "audio_file": "mp3/beat-B01.mp3",
      "actual_duration_s": 13.53,
      "build": {
        "status": "VIDEO",
        "src": "media/B01.mp4",
        "filled_by": "media",
        "at": "2026-07-18T23:16:19"
      }
    },
    {
      "beat_id": "B02",
      "estimated_duration_s": 10,
      "narration_text": "I does nothing. X flips zero and one. Z changes their relative phase. Y combines bit and phase action, up to phase conventions.",
      "shot": {
        "type": "GRAPHIC",
        "source": "own",
        "motion": "branch"
      },
      "audio_file": "mp3/beat-B02.mp3",
      "actual_duration_s": 8.58,
      "build": {
        "status": "MANIM",
        "src": "manim/B02.mp4",
        "filled_by": "manim",
        "at": "2026-07-18T23:16:19"
      }
    },
    {
      "beat_id": "B03",
      "estimated_duration_s": 11,
      "narration_text": "Together I, X, Y, and Z are linearly independent and span every two-by-two complex matrix. Any linear operator on one qubit can be written as a weighted sum of them.",
      "shot": {
        "type": "GRAPHIC",
        "source": "own",
        "motion": "accumulate"
      },
      "audio_file": "mp3/beat-B03.mp3",
      "actual_duration_s": 10.54,
      "build": {
        "status": "MANIM",
        "src": "manim/B03.mp4",
        "filled_by": "manim",
        "at": "2026-07-18T23:16:19"
      }
    },
    {
      "beat_id": "B04",
      "estimated_duration_s": 10,
      "narration_text": "For example, a small rotation about a tilted axis looks continuous, but its operator is still a combination of I and Pauli terms.",
      "shot": {
        "type": "GRAPHIC",
        "source": "own",
        "motion": "morph"
      },
      "audio_file": "mp3/beat-B04.mp3",
      "actual_duration_s": 8.3,
      "build": {
        "status": "MANIM",
        "src": "manim/B04.mp4",
        "filled_by": "manim",
        "at": "2026-07-18T23:16:19"
      }
    },
    {
      "beat_id": "B05",
      "estimated_duration_s": 11,
      "narration_text": "This does not mean nature secretly chooses one Pauli error before measurement. A coherent error can remain a superposition of different error actions.",
      "shot": {
        "type": "GRAPHIC",
        "source": "own",
        "motion": "compare"
      },
      "audio_file": "mp3/beat-B05.mp3",
      "actual_duration_s": 9.0,
      "build": {
        "status": "MANIM",
        "src": "manim/B05.mp4",
        "filled_by": "manim",
        "at": "2026-07-18T23:16:19"
      }
    },
    {
      "beat_id": "B06",
      "estimated_duration_s": 11,
      "narration_text": "A suitable code correlates correctable error components with syndrome subspaces. Measuring the syndrome can then remove coherence between distinguishable syndrome outcomes.",
      "shot": {
        "type": "GRAPHIC",
        "source": "own",
        "motion": "collapse"
      },
      "audio_file": "mp3/beat-B06.mp3",
      "actual_duration_s": 10.18,
      "build": {
        "status": "MANIM",
        "src": "manim/B06.mp4",
        "filled_by": "manim",
        "at": "2026-07-18T23:16:19"
      }
    },
    {
      "beat_id": "B07",
      "estimated_duration_s": 11,
      "narration_text": "If the syndrome identifies X, the recovery reverses X. If it identifies Z or Y, the corresponding recovery is applied. Degenerate codes may assign different errors the same syndrome when they act equivalently on encoded information.",
      "shot": {
        "type": "GRAPHIC",
        "source": "own",
        "motion": "branch"
      },
      "audio_file": "mp3/beat-B07.mp3",
      "actual_duration_s": 14.31,
      "build": {
        "status": "MANIM",
        "src": "manim/B07.mp4",
        "filled_by": "manim",
        "at": "2026-07-18T23:16:19"
      }
    },
    {
      "beat_id": "B08",
      "estimated_duration_s": 11,
      "narration_text": "The deeper principle is linearity: if a code corrects a set of basis errors under the error-correction conditions, it also corrects every operator in their linear span.",
      "shot": {
        "type": "GRAPHIC",
        "source": "own",
        "motion": "accumulate"
      },
      "audio_file": "mp3/beat-B08.mp3",
      "actual_duration_s": 9.41,
      "build": {
        "status": "MANIM",
        "src": "manim/B08.mp4",
        "filled_by": "manim",
        "at": "2026-07-18T23:16:19"
      }
    },
    {
      "beat_id": "B09",
      "estimated_duration_s": 11,
      "narration_text": "Real noise can also be probabilistic and entangle the qubit with an environment. The Pauli basis still organizes its single-qubit operator components, but it is not a claim that every noise channel is one unitary Pauli.",
      "shot": {
        "type": "GRAPHIC",
        "source": "own",
        "motion": "compare"
      },
      "audio_file": "mp3/beat-B09.mp3",
      "actual_duration_s": 13.35,
      "build": {
        "status": "MANIM",
        "src": "manim/B09.mp4",
        "filled_by": "manim",
        "at": "2026-07-18T23:16:19"
      }
    },
    {
      "beat_id": "B10",
      "estimated_duration_s": 10,
      "narration_text": "So four does not replace infinity by magic. Four supplies coordinates for the space of one-qubit operators, and the code's linear correction property does the rest.",
      "shot": {
        "type": "GRAPHIC",
        "source": "own",
        "motion": "transform"
      },
      "audio_file": "mp3/beat-B10.mp3",
      "actual_duration_s": 9.69,
      "build": {
        "status": "MANIM",
        "src": "manim/B10.mp4",
        "filled_by": "manim",
        "at": "2026-07-18T23:16:19"
      }
    },
    {
      "beat_id": "B11",
      "estimated_duration_s": 12,
      "narration_text": "Your turn. A tiny rotation is not exactly X, Y, or Z. Why can a code correcting the Pauli basis still handle it? Pause before the answer: expand the rotation operator in that basis, then use linearity of correction.",
      "shot": {
        "type": "CARD",
        "source": "remotion",
        "motion": "type",
        "remotion": {
          "pattern": "ClaudeComposerAsk",
          "props": {
            "command": "Your turn: connect a tiny rotation to Pauli correction.",
            "topic": "QUANTUM MECHANICS — CHECK",
            "segment": "Basis, Not Lottery",
            "greeting": "Over to you, Liam",
            "runningText": "waiting for the linearity argument…",
            "output": [
              "Expand the rotation in I, X, Y, Z.",
              "Correction of the basis extends to its correctable span."
            ],
            "folderLabel": "@NikBearBrown",
            "modelLabel": "Claude",
            "effortLabel": "High"
          },
          "rendered": {
            "out": "media/B11.mp4",
            "at": ""
          }
        }
      },
      "audio_file": "mp3/beat-B11.mp3",
      "actual_duration_s": 13.08,
      "build": {
        "status": "VIDEO",
        "src": "media/B11.mp4",
        "filled_by": "media",
        "at": "2026-07-18T23:16:19"
      }
    },
    {
      "beat_id": "B12",
      "estimated_duration_s": 8,
      "narration_text": "Every one-qubit operator is built from four: identity, bit flip, phase flip, and their Y-shaped combination.",
      "shot": {
        "type": "GRAPHIC",
        "source": "own",
        "motion": "fade"
      },
      "audio_file": "mp3/beat-B12.mp3",
      "actual_duration_s": 6.85,
